Where do these thoughts, exercises, and outputs take my interest in chairs? [[Chris Attenborough]]'s interest in chairs? Our interest in sculpture, building, art in general, design being practical, art sort of questioning or critiquing or just making fun of our realities sometimes?

Seed from [[What else is a chair for?]]. See [[Everyone Wants to Design a Chair]].

!! From the Sep 25, 2026 build session

Talking with Chris while we built. Recordings and cleaned transcripts are in the chair-ness repo, `text/voice-memo-2026-09-25-*`.

* We've made a lot of scrap wood ones. Where else do other materials come in? Felt, even if it's just cushioning on top.
* Found parts that are already the right shape. A bent IKEA under-mattress support is already a really big swoopy lounge chair. Scrap wood fills in the structural gaps. More of the parts have to be found.
* A fine woodworking version of each chair. "Oh, I didn't think about the space, there's going to be a gap here, so I just put in a chunk of wood." Next version: a lap joint there, or a brass rod through it.
* What do you do with them when you don't need them? Shaker chairs had handles in the upper backs and struts made to hang on pegs on the wall ([[Shaker Ladder Back]]). Is there a system or an intention for how ours live in a space?
* Keep the variation. The time limit is what makes it. Chris sees the same thing in the ad hoc chairs of Iceland: people limited to what they had still got the thing together ([[Adhocism]], [[Satisfice]]).
* Building small teaches the joinery fast. [[Gerrit Rietveld]] built at right angles because it's the quickest way to bolt or nail things together ([[Red Blue Chair]]). We only saw that once we were doing it ourselves.

We'd joked that the sixteen chairs were really a musical chairs set. That weekend, nine of them were used for musical chairs at Union's Oktoberfest party. We picked the safest ones, the least likely to tip over under sudden body motion.
